Subject: dts: be7000: keep the CPU rail at 875 mV or more like stock

The SoC OPP table runs the CPUs at 725 to 862.5 mV up to 1.416 GHz and
the s1 regulator may go down to 725 mV. Stock never goes below the
875 mV the bootloader sets, its CPR keeps every frequency at or above
it. A newer BE7000 hung at random with our table, early in boot and on
reboot, with nothing in the logs. Keep the same frequencies with the
stock floor: s1 minimum 875 mV, the four lower OPPs at 875 mV.

--- a/target/linux/qualcommbe/dts/ipq9574-be7000.dts
+++ b/target/linux/qualcommbe/dts/ipq9574-be7000.dts
@@ -336,8 +336,12 @@
 			 * Update the regulator-min-microvolt with SVS voltage of 725mV so that
 			 * the regulators are brought up with 725mV which is sufficient for all the
 			 * corner parts to operate at 800MHz
+			 *
+			 * Not on this board: some BE7000 parts hang at random with the
+			 * CPU rail that low. Stock (CPR) never takes it below the 875mV
+			 * the bootloader sets, so neither do we.
 			 */
-			regulator-min-microvolt = <725000>;
+			regulator-min-microvolt = <875000>;
 			regulator-max-microvolt = <1075000>;
 		};
 	};
@@ -1035,3 +1039,26 @@
 		};
 	};
 };
+
+/*
+ * The SoC table runs 936 MHz to 1.416 GHz at 725 to 862.5 mV. Stock keeps
+ * the CPU rail at 875 mV or more whatever the clock, and some newer BE7000
+ * parts hang at random below that. Same frequencies, stock floor.
+ */
+&cpu_opp_table {
+	opp-936000000 {
+		opp-microvolt = <875000>;
+	};
+
+	opp-1104000000 {
+		opp-microvolt = <875000>;
+	};
+
+	opp-1200000000 {
+		opp-microvolt = <875000>;
+	};
+
+	opp-1416000000 {
+		opp-microvolt = <875000>;
+	};
+};
